\ ======================================================================
\ 06 — failing, and recovering.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D-71): aborting is not a control-flow primitive. `Fail` is an
\ effect with one operation, `try`/`catch` is its handler, and `!Fail` in a
\ row means "this may not return normally" the same way `!IO` means "this may
\ print". It composes with everything in demos 02–05 because it is not
\ special.
\
\ This is also the demo with the most honest gaps in it, and they are all one
\ gap: failure carries no value yet.
\ ======================================================================

\ --- Unhandled, `fail` escapes to the top like any other operation.

fail

\ --- `try { … } catch { … }` discharges it. The row of the whole expression
\ has no `!Fail` in it.

define validate ( $n:i64 -- i64 !Fail ) { $n 0 < if { } then { fail 0 } else { $n } endif }

try { 7 validate } catch { 0 } show print " " print
try { -7 validate } catch { 0 } show print "\n" print

\ --- A LIMIT, up front, because it shapes everything below. A `try` block
\ runs on a FRESH stack: it cannot reach what was already there, and a local
\ is a stack slot, so it cannot reach one of those either. The value to be
\ validated has to be produced INSIDE the block, which is why both lines above
\ carry their own literal.

define or_zero ( $n:i64 -- i64 ) { try { $n validate } catch { 0 } }

\ The core does not require this. The elaborator's stack model cannot compute
\ how deep a block reached, so it gives the block nothing rather than guessing.
\ It is the single biggest thing standing between `try` and ordinary use.

\ Look at `validate`'s body: `fail 0`. `fail` is `( -- !Fail )`, so it cannot
\ stand where a value is expected, and the two branches of an `if` must agree
\ on what they leave behind. The `0` is dead — nothing runs after `fail` — and
\ it is there only to make the branch types line up. What is wanted is a `fail`
\ at the empty type, `( -- #A !Fail )`; the core already HAS the empty type
\ (`TSum []`, uninhabited) and generics now exist, so this is a small change
\ that has not been made. See U01 §9.

\ --- Everything the try block built is discarded, so `catch` takes no inputs
\ and must leave what the try block would have left.

try { 1 2 3 + + fail } catch { -1 } show print "\n" print

\ --- Disagreeing about that is what gets rejected:

try { 42 } catch { "nope" }

\ ======================================================================
\ Composing
\ ======================================================================

effect Log { declare say ( str -- ) }

\ A word that both logs and may fail carries both:

define risky ( $n:i64 -- i64 !Log !Fail ) {
  "  checking\n" say
  $n 100 < if { } then { $n } else { "  too big\n" say fail 0 } endif
}

\ Handle them in either order — they are independent rows, not a monad stack
\ that has to be assembled in the right sequence.

define small ( -- i64 ) {
  handle Log over ( ) init { } { say { print } } { try { 42 risky } catch { -1 } }
}

define big ( -- i64 ) {
  handle Log over ( ) init { } { say { print } } { try { 999 risky } catch { -1 } }
}

"(a) 42:\n" print small show print "\n" print
"(b) 999:\n" print big show print "\n" print

\ Note that (b) printed "checking" and "too big" before failing. The log
\ survives the abort because the handler is OUTSIDE the `try`: the abort cuts
\ the stack back, not the effects already performed. Put the handler inside
\ and the captured log would be discarded with everything else.

\ --- And with the Dictionary: `validate` is an ordinary word, so demo 03's
\ rebinding replaces the whole failing policy from outside.

define permissive ( i64 -- i64 ) { }

define lenient ( -- i64 ) { with { validate permissive } { try { -7 validate } catch { 0 } } }

lenient show print "\n" print

\ ======================================================================
\ The gaps, named
\ ======================================================================

\ 1. `fail` takes no payload and `catch` receives none, so a failure cannot
\    say what went wrong. That is why the prelude's `parse` returns 0 on bad
\    input and `getenv` returns "" when unset: their honest signatures are
\    `( str -- i64 !Fail )` and `( str -- str !Fail )`, and the reason they
\    still return sentinels is not that failure is unavailable but that a
\    caller who WANTS the sentinel has no way to ask for it back.

"not a number" parse show print "\n" print

\ 2. The fresh-stack rule from the top of this file, restated as the error you
\    actually hit:

define reaches ( i64 -- i64 ) { try { 1 + } catch { 0 } }

\ 3. An abort discards values without consulting their capabilities, so a
\    linear value in flight when a `fail` fires is leaked rather than
\    released. Tracked as Q-17 in NOTES/N02_Open_Questions.md. Nothing
\    demonstrates it yet, because no surface word constructs a `Box`.
